# appkit — environment manifest
#
# Copy to `.env` for local dev; set the same names in your host's env UI
# (Vercel: Project → Settings → Environment Variables) for deploys.
#
# Every variable below is read by `api/*.ts` at request time. NONE of them are
# `VITE_`-prefixed, and that is deliberate: this kit ships no browser env vars
# at all. Client-side values (publishable key, buy-button id, payment URL)
# live in `kit.config.ts`, which is bundled and public by design.
#
#   >> Anything you prefix with `VITE_` is compiled into the JavaScript you
#   >> serve to every visitor. No secret below may ever wear that prefix.
#
# Verified against the code 2026-07-31: this file lists exactly the six vars
# `process.env` is read for, and no others.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Required — client mode (the default; no backend store)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Entitlement lives in the buyer's browser and is re-verified against Stripe on
# demand. These three are all you need to take money.
#
# Read by: api/verify-purchase.ts, api/stripe-webhook.ts

# Stripe API secret. Server-only, full account access.
# Use sk_test_... against test-mode prices and sk_live_... against live ones —
# a key and a price from different modes will not match, and the failure looks
# like "the buyer paid but Pro didn't unlock."
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_REPLACE_ME

# The price and product a paid session must contain to count as Pro. Either one
# matching is enough (Stripe rotates prices under a stable product for promos).
# Both must come from the same Stripe mode as STRIPE_SECRET_KEY above.
STRIPE_PRICE_ID=price_REPLACE_ME
STRIPE_PRODUCT_ID=prod_REPLACE_ME


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional — server-entitlement mode (durable, refund-aware)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Setting these upgrades the app to a durable entitlement store with NO code
# change: the webhook records each paid session, refunds flip the row to
# `refunded`, and restore-by-email reads the table instead of scanning Stripe.
#
# ###########################################################################
# ##  SET ALL THREE OR NONE. Two of the three is worse than zero.          ##
# ###########################################################################
#
# `serverModeEnabled()` (api/_lib.ts) checks only SUPABASE_URL and
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY. So if you set those two and forget
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET:
#
#   1. Server mode reports ON, so restore-by-email treats the `entitlements`
#      table as the source of truth and no longer falls back to Stripe.
#   2. The webhook, missing its secret, returns 200 `{handled: false}` and
#      writes nothing — so the table stays permanently empty. Stripe sees a
#      successful delivery, so there is no failed-webhook alert to notice.
#   3. Every restore therefore answers "no purchase found" to customers who
#      genuinely paid, and the Stripe fallback that would have rescued them is
#      skipped *because* server mode is on.
#
# It is silent end to end: no error, no retry, no log. Set all three together,
# then buy one Pro seat and restore it by email before you trust the config.
#
# Read by: api/_lib.ts (first two), api/stripe-webhook.ts (all three)

# Supabase project URL, e.g. https://<project-ref>.supabase.co
SUPABASE_URL=

# Service-role key. BYPASSES ROW LEVEL SECURITY — full read/write on every
# table. Server-only, never `VITE_`, never in the client bundle, never in a
# repo. Rotate it if it is ever pasted anywhere it can be read back.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# Signing secret for the /api/stripe-webhook route (Stripe → Webhooks →
# your endpoint → Signing secret). Mode-specific: the `stripe listen` secret
# for local dev is NOT the same string as your deployed endpoint's.
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Test vs live: the hygiene rule
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Keep whole sets together. Every variable in a given environment should come
# from the same Stripe mode and the same Supabase project:
#
#   Preview / staging  → sk_test_..., test price + product, test webhook
#                        secret, a non-production Supabase project
#   Production         → sk_live_..., live price + product, the live
#                        endpoint's webhook secret, the production project
#
# Mixing modes fails in the least helpful way available: the money arrives and
# the unlock does not. If you take one thing from this file, take that the
# expensive failures here are silent ones.
